\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Explain why the curve shown never crosses the $x$-axis or the $y$-axis.

foundation.solution_text:
As $x$ gets larger, $y=\dfrac{k}{x}$ gets smaller and smaller but never reaches $0$, so the curve never touches the $x$-axis.
As $x$ gets closer to $0$, $y$ gets larger and larger without limit, so the curve never touches the $y$-axis either.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=0.5:4.5,smooth,samples=60,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{2.2/\t});
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,1.1) {$y\to 0$};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (0.85,4.0) {$x\to 0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

